WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.293
Welcome!

00:00:01.293 --> 00:00:04.553
In this Java tutorial, you'll handle exceptions,

00:00:04.553 --> 00:00:07.066
the objects that describe interrupted

00:00:07.066 --> 00:00:10.190
operations, and use a stack trace, a record of

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.499
method calls, to locate a failure.

00:00:13.165 --> 00:00:16.765
Exception handling helps applications respond to

00:00:16.765 --> 00:00:20.215
failed requests without losing unrelated work,

00:00:20.215 --> 00:00:23.665
while diagnostic traces help developers locate

00:00:23.665 --> 00:00:25.915
the code that needs attention.

00:00:26.568 --> 00:00:29.977
Today we'll build a calculator for campus staff

00:00:29.977 --> 00:00:33.169
comparing ways to divide twelve supply items

00:00:33.169 --> 00:00:34.112
among groups.

00:00:34.112 --> 00:00:37.449
The proposed group counts are three, zero, and

00:00:37.449 --> 00:00:40.858
four; each proposal starts with the same twelve

00:00:40.858 --> 00:00:41.293
items.

00:00:41.936 --> 00:00:45.278
A valid proposal should report items per group.

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:48.264
Zero groups should receive a clear failure

00:00:48.264 --> 00:00:51.677
message, and the next proposal should still run.

00:00:51.677 --> 00:00:54.734
We'll also trace where a failed method call

00:00:54.734 --> 00:00:55.161
began.

00:00:55.826 --> 00:00:58.726
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:00:58.726 --> 00:00:59.401
Main.java.

00:01:03.076 --> 00:01:04.290
The file is open.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.718
We'll separate calculating a share from deciding

00:01:07.718 --> 00:01:09.289
what to tell the user.

00:01:09.289 --> 00:01:12.288
Let's write RatioTools with a static share

00:01:12.288 --> 00:01:15.573
method that receives an item total and a group

00:01:15.573 --> 00:01:16.001
count.

00:01:24.849 --> 00:01:27.446
Share returns whole items per group.

00:01:27.446 --> 00:01:30.764
With twelve items and three groups, it returns

00:01:30.764 --> 00:01:31.124
four.

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:34.082
With zero groups, integer division raises

00:01:34.082 --> 00:01:37.472
ArithmeticException before a number can return.

00:01:37.472 --> 00:01:40.934
This is a failure during execution; the method's

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.449
source is valid Java.

00:01:42.458 --> 00:01:45.731
To examine a chain of calls later, let's add

00:01:45.731 --> 00:01:46.177
quote.

00:01:46.177 --> 00:01:49.302
Here a quote means a proposed share, not a

00:01:49.302 --> 00:01:49.748
price.

00:01:49.748 --> 00:01:53.244
This small helper requests twelve items divided

00:01:53.244 --> 00:01:54.583
among zero groups.

00:02:00.246 --> 00:02:03.505
Quote calls share rather than doing the division

00:02:03.505 --> 00:02:03.980
itself.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.764
That gives us a caller to look for in the

00:02:06.764 --> 00:02:07.782
recorded trace.

00:02:07.782 --> 00:02:10.906
Our first comparison will call share directly,

00:02:10.906 --> 00:02:12.671
so quote does not run yet.

00:02:12.683 --> 00:02:15.953
Now let's add the program's starting structure

00:02:15.953 --> 00:02:18.086
and its proposed group counts.

00:02:18.086 --> 00:02:21.427
Main is the class that contains this standalone

00:02:21.427 --> 00:02:24.626
program; its main method is where Java begins

00:02:24.626 --> 00:02:25.408
running it.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.122
The array holds three independent proposals.

00:02:40.122 --> 00:02:43.264
The loop supplies one group count at a time,

00:02:43.264 --> 00:02:45.549
while the total stays at twelve.

00:02:45.549 --> 00:02:48.477
These are alternative plans, so we do not

00:02:48.477 --> 00:02:50.905
subtract items as we compare them.

00:02:50.917 --> 00:02:54.467
Each proposal needs its own response if division

00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:54.911
fails.

00:02:54.911 --> 00:02:57.795
Let's put a try block inside this loop,

00:02:57.795 --> 00:03:00.901
containing the method call and the success

00:03:00.901 --> 00:03:01.567
messages.

00:03:14.668 --> 00:03:17.570
Try marks the work whose failure the following

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:18.832
handler can address.

00:03:18.832 --> 00:03:21.671
Java must finish the share call before it can

00:03:21.671 --> 00:03:24.006
assemble and print the Share message.

00:03:24.006 --> 00:03:26.908
If the call fails, that print never completes,

00:03:26.908 --> 00:03:29.558
and the Calculated message is skipped too.

00:03:29.558 --> 00:03:32.207
Leaving unfinished work this way is called

00:03:32.207 --> 00:03:33.343
abrupt completion.

00:03:33.363 --> 00:03:36.522
We need to explain the failed proposal without

00:03:36.522 --> 00:03:38.650
pretending it produced a share.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.878
Let's add a catch block for ArithmeticException

00:03:41.878 --> 00:03:43.663
and print a clear message.

00:03:55.817 --> 00:03:57.298
Catch is the handler.

00:03:57.298 --> 00:04:00.401
Its variable problem refers to the exception

00:04:00.401 --> 00:04:03.151
object produced by the failed division.

00:04:03.151 --> 00:04:06.254
The handler reports the problem; it does not

00:04:06.254 --> 00:04:07.947
invent a numeric answer.

00:04:07.947 --> 00:04:10.768
Because this handler belongs to one loop

00:04:10.768 --> 00:04:14.012
iteration, a zero count does not discard later

00:04:14.012 --> 00:04:14.717
proposals.

00:04:14.739 --> 00:04:17.638
Finally, let's mark the end of each attempt

00:04:17.638 --> 00:04:20.807
inside the loop, then print Done once after all

00:04:20.807 --> 00:04:22.964
the inputs have been considered.

00:04:31.551 --> 00:04:34.697
Next input is after the handler but still inside

00:04:34.697 --> 00:04:35.287
the loop.

00:04:35.287 --> 00:04:38.433
It can print after success or a handled failure.

00:04:38.433 --> 00:04:41.316
Done is outside the loop, so it prints once.

00:04:41.316 --> 00:04:44.134
Finishing catch continues after the try and

00:04:44.134 --> 00:04:47.280
catch statement; it does not retry the division.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.426
Let's click Run and examine the complete report.

00:04:55.507 --> 00:04:58.193
The first proposal prints Share: four, then

00:04:58.193 --> 00:05:01.066
Calculated, because twelve divided among three

00:05:01.066 --> 00:05:02.065
groups succeeds.

00:05:02.065 --> 00:05:04.127
Next input finishes that attempt.

00:05:04.127 --> 00:05:07.000
Zero groups prints the failure message instead

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.998
of either success line, then reaches Next input.

00:05:09.998 --> 00:05:12.684
Four groups still gets its turn and reports

00:05:12.684 --> 00:05:14.058
three items per group.

00:05:14.058 --> 00:05:15.807
Done appears after the loop.

00:05:15.859 --> 00:05:19.012
We handled one failed proposal while preserving

00:05:19.012 --> 00:05:20.287
the remaining work.

00:05:20.287 --> 00:05:23.038
A handler around the entire loop would be

00:05:23.038 --> 00:05:26.124
reached only after that loop had been left, so

00:05:26.124 --> 00:05:29.009
it would not resume at the following input.

00:05:29.055 --> 00:05:32.454
Let's test the same rule when the failure comes

00:05:32.454 --> 00:05:32.888
first.

00:05:32.888 --> 00:05:36.215
We'll change only the proposed counts to zero,

00:05:36.215 --> 00:05:39.181
six, and two, leaving the methods and all

00:05:39.181 --> 00:05:40.555
messages unchanged.

00:05:45.149 --> 00:05:48.106
Before running, predict every output line in

00:05:48.106 --> 00:05:48.510
order.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:51.602
Decide which messages the zero count skips and

00:05:51.602 --> 00:05:54.492
whether both later proposals are processed.

00:05:54.492 --> 00:05:57.584
Remember that each proposal uses twelve items.

00:05:57.584 --> 00:06:00.474
Pause here to work through your prediction.

00:06:08.527 --> 00:06:11.761
Let's click Run and compare the output with your

00:06:11.761 --> 00:06:12.502
prediction.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.520
The first input reaches the handler, then Next

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:20.922
input.

00:06:20.922 --> 00:06:23.734
Six groups receive two items each, and two

00:06:23.734 --> 00:06:25.743
groups receive six items each.

00:06:25.743 --> 00:06:28.622
Both successful proposals print Calculated.

00:06:28.622 --> 00:06:31.367
A failure at the beginning still does not

00:06:31.367 --> 00:06:34.581
prevent either later iteration, because recovery

00:06:34.581 --> 00:06:36.590
happens within each iteration.

00:06:36.636 --> 00:06:39.429
That explains how processing continues.

00:06:39.429 --> 00:06:42.508
A developer also needs to locate the failed

00:06:42.508 --> 00:06:45.588
operation, especially when one method calls

00:06:45.588 --> 00:06:46.161
another.

00:06:46.161 --> 00:06:49.527
We'll keep RatioTools and replace the loop with

00:06:49.527 --> 00:06:52.463
a call to quote, followed by a diagnostic

00:06:52.463 --> 00:06:53.036
handler.

00:07:24.556 --> 00:07:26.911
Quote calls share with zero groups.

00:07:26.911 --> 00:07:30.140
Share cannot return a quotient, and quote cannot

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:31.620
finish its own return.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:34.781
Java leaves those unfinished calls to reach our

00:07:34.781 --> 00:07:35.320
handler.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:37.943
That outward search is stack unwinding.

00:07:37.943 --> 00:07:41.172
Quote completed is skipped because its preceding

00:07:41.172 --> 00:07:43.056
call never returns normally.

00:07:43.093 --> 00:07:45.731
GetMessage reads the diagnostic message.

00:07:45.731 --> 00:07:48.500
GetStackTrace returns an array of recorded

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:48.896
calls.

00:07:48.896 --> 00:07:51.468
Index zero selects the first entry, and

00:07:51.468 --> 00:07:54.501
getMethodName reads its method name; index one

00:07:54.501 --> 00:07:55.886
reads the next entry.

00:07:55.886 --> 00:07:57.666
Both exist in this example.

00:07:57.666 --> 00:08:00.766
General diagnostic code should check the length

00:08:00.766 --> 00:08:02.480
before selecting an entry.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:05.118
Let's click Run to inspect this failure.

00:08:10.137 --> 00:08:12.609
The message identifies division by zero.

00:08:12.609 --> 00:08:15.389
Share appears first because that is where the

00:08:15.389 --> 00:08:16.378
division failed.

00:08:16.378 --> 00:08:19.034
Quote appears next because it called share.

00:08:19.034 --> 00:08:21.753
We entered quote before share, but the trace

00:08:21.753 --> 00:08:24.225
begins at the failure and works outward.

00:08:24.225 --> 00:08:27.067
No quotient traveled back through these calls;

00:08:27.067 --> 00:08:29.662
the exception reached the handler instead.

00:08:30.368 --> 00:08:33.074
You used try and catch to give a failed request

00:08:33.074 --> 00:08:35.435
a defined response, placed the handler so

00:08:35.435 --> 00:08:37.911
independent work could continue, and used a

00:08:37.911 --> 00:08:40.271
stack trace to locate the failure and its

00:08:40.271 --> 00:08:40.674
caller.

00:08:40.674 --> 00:08:43.381
In another application, consider which failures

00:08:43.381 --> 00:08:46.087
should reject just one request and which should

00:08:46.087 --> 00:08:47.526
stop the whole operation.

00:08:47.526 --> 00:08:50.232
That requirement should guide where you put the

00:08:50.232 --> 00:08:50.693
handler.
